@prefix ds: <https://ds.canonical.com/>.
@prefix anatomy: <https://anatomy.canonical.com/>.
@prefix dt: <https://dt.canonical.com/>.

ds:concept.Design-tokens-Color---explanation a ds:Concept;
    ds:name "Design tokens: Color - explanation";
    ds:summary "This document describes Pragma’s approach to defining color paletted and the corresponding color design tokens. ​These design tokens are meant to be used across the entire design system as well as for brand purposes.  \nFor the general Design tokens specification go to [Design tokens: Overview](https://docs.superhuman.com/d/_dNyzE_TLZDh#_tugrid-GnfOU3MM-3/_rui-BlNN-e8ieQ).  \nFor referencing the list of color tokens go to [Design tokens: Color - reference](https://docs.superhuman.com/d/_dNyzE_TLZDh#_tugrid-GnfOU3MM-3/_rui-UTIx5gBv57) \n\nColor palettes in UI design serve as comprehensive collections of colors that support diverse UI requirements. They should provide enough range to accommodate different use cases and to create different themes - including light, dark and high-contrast versions - all while maintaining visual consistency. Therefore it is common practice to include more colors than are immediately needed to accommodate future growth of the design system and evolving product and brand requirements. \n\n​This color palette and corresponding set of design tokens will emphasize color contrast and accessibility requirements. This approach is intended to achieve three main objectives:\n\n1. Simplify the selection of colors (picking one instead of having to create one)\n2. Facilitate the easy creation of accessible color combinations that meet contrast requirements\n3. Create a systematic color framework to improve overall visual consistency";
    ds:tier ds:global;
    ds:content "## Requirements\n\n## **Consistency**\n\nFor colors to look consistent across an entire palette and for our contrast rules to work properly, the lightness within a step must be approximately the same. This means that if there are steps such as 5, 10, 15 ... 85, 90, 95, each color within the step of e.g. 50 must have approximately the same lightness.\n\n​\n\nThe saturation curve should also be consistent across the entire palette. However, the saturation scale can be increased or decreased for specific colors or color groups to accommodate different use cases such as semantic colors (which should generally be more saturated) or brand colors (which can be less saturated). So while the absolute saturation may vary, the relative rate at which the saturation changes should ideally remain the same.\n\n​\n\n\n\nFig.: Two saturation curves. The red curve is scaled down to have a lower absolute saturation. \n\nBut the relative rate of saturation changes is the same so colors look consistent.\n\n​\n\nWe will also have to have some tolerances as not every hue is able to exhibit every possible lightness and saturation. For example, there is no dark yellow.\n\n## **Hues**\n\nFor the first version we will create shades for the following [hues](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hue). These colors are inspired by the current state of colors in the Design system. \n\n​\n\nTable 86\n\n​\n\n## **Saturation**\n\nFor the first version of the color palette we will go with a [saturation](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Colorfulness) curve that for the most part follows the “natural” saturation curve of colors where the extreme ends are less saturated and the middle is more saturated. This saturation curve can be adjusted by setting a minimum and maximum saturation. The following min and max values have been chosen for the colors:\n\n​\n\nTable 87\n\n​\n\n## **Lightness**\n\nThe most easily recognizable difference between shade steps is lightness. The lightness between steps is determined by the contrast requirements described in the following paragraphs, as the lightness contrast (which is the contrast that affects accessibility) is, as the name suggests, determined by the difference in lightness of a color pair.\n\n#### Accessibility & contrast\n\n**Law**\n\n[Many countries around the world have laws related to web accessibility](https://www.w3.org/WAI/policies/). While these laws do not always (but sometimes) apply to the private sector, they do have an impact on the public sector and therefore on the procurement of services by public sector organizations from private sector companies. Accessibility should of course be pursued in all cases, but in some countries it may also be a legal requirement or a condition for public sector organizations to become customers.\n\nThis means that we need to abide by [WCAG 2.* AA](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG2AA-Conformance) rules in our UIs. For this color palette work the following WCAG 2.2 success criterion are applicable:\n\n- [1.4.3 Contrast](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG20/Understanding/contrast-minimum#:~:text=%3A%20Contrast%20(Minimum)-,Success%20Criterion%201.4.,Large%20Text)\n  - The visual presentation of[ text](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/Understanding/contrast-minimum.html#dfn-text) and[ images of text](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/Understanding/contrast-minimum.html#dfn-image-of-text) has a[ contrast ratio](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/Understanding/contrast-minimum.html#dfn-contrast-ratio) of at least 4.5:1\n- [1.4.11 Non-text Contrast](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/Understanding/non-text-contrast.html)\n  - The visual[ presentation](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/Understanding/non-text-contrast#dfn-presentation) of the following have a[ contrast ratio](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/Understanding/non-text-contrast#dfn-contrast-ratio) of at least 3:1 against adjacent color(s):\n    - User Interface Components: visual information required to identify[ user interface components](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/Understanding/non-text-contrast#dfn-user-interface-component) and[ states](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/Understanding/non-text-contrast#dfn-state), except for inactive components or where the appearance of the component is determined by the user agent and not modified by the author;\n    - Graphical Objects: parts of graphics required to understand the content, except when a particular presentation of graphics is[ essential](https://www.w3.org/WAI/WCAG21/Understanding/non-text-contrast#dfn-essential) to the information being conveyed.\n\n  \n\n\n**WCAG contrast algorithm vs APCA**\n\nUnfortunately the [contrast algorithm in WCAG 2](https://www.w3.org/TR/UNDERSTANDING-WCAG20/visual-audio-contrast-contrast.html#visual-audio-contrast-contrast-intent-head:~:text=is%20much%20larger.-,Notes%20on%20formula,-Conversion%20from%20nonlinear) (which is based on [ISO-9241-3-1992](https://cdn.standards.iteh.ai/samples/16875/4e1bf8a0a2d34083a45e301f1e30884c/ISO-9241-3-1992.pdf)) is[ quite flawed](https://github.com/Myndex/SAPC-APCA/discussions/30). It has both false negatives and false positives. Meaning it passes colors that do not have enough contrast and doesn’t pass some colors that do have enough contrast. A contrast algorithm evidently better at actually predicting human perceived color contrast is [APCA](https://git.apcacontrast.com/documentation/APCAeasyIntro.html).\n\nThis puts us in a dilemma. On the one hand, we want to be as accessible as possible and therefore use APCA to determine contrast as people actually perceive it. On the other hand, of course, we want to comply with the law and avoid unnecessary discussions with customers about the details of accessibility.\n\n​\n\nAfter having tested an APCA based color palette in [DE024 - Color palette](https://docs.google.com/document/u/0/d/14qB9_j_A2Th-t-vzma-CFKleSSXRvwLHg4oUw1AvhX0/edit) we came to the conclusion that a WCAG based color palette fits our needs better. After all, we need to be following the WCAG contrast requirements and trying to follow both APCA and WCAG diminishes the ease of use for this palette and makes the color pairs so dark (to meet both APCA and WCAG) that the resulting color pairs are undesirable from a branding perspective.  \n\n**Improving the experience of selecting an accessible contrast**\n\nAs part of this specification we did [internal user research](https://miro.com/app/board/uXjVL2kS6jk=/) and one of the biggest identified pain points when having to select a color for e.g. a new component is the hassle of having to verify it meets the above mentioned accessibility requirements. That's why, for this new color palette, we want to make accessible contrast guarantees a core of the new color palette and thereby improve the experience of selecting accessible color pairs.\n\n​\n\nWe aim to achieve this by providing the following contrast guarantees:\n\n- Shade steps that are 400 or further apart have\n  - A WCAG contrast of at least 3:1\n- Shade steps that are 500 or further apart have\n  - A WCAG contrast of at least 4.5:1\n- Shade steps that are 700 or further apart have\n  - A WCAG contrast of at least 8:1\n\n  \n\n\nThis way it is possible to select any color in the palette as a background and be able to pick a foreground/text color that is guaranteed to meet the required accessibility/contrast standards.\n\n## **Shade steps**\n\nThe number of shades for each hue presents a challenge. As outlined before we want to provide more colors than are immediately needed to accommodate future growth of the design system and evolving product and brand requirements. On the other hand too many shades disrupt visual hierarchy and consistency, impacting both design decisions and user experience.\n\n​\n\nThe palette was built with a [magic number scale](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1G45-Q3hUDhD6X7B6tSZiiME4euANxsyVdfS4pt9899w/edit?tab=t.0#heading=h.jshd15law1cf) as described in the [DE015 - Design Tokens](https://docs.google.com/document/u/0/d/1G45-Q3hUDhD6X7B6tSZiiME4euANxsyVdfS4pt9899w/edit) specification. This gives us the flexibility to add additional shades in the future if the need arises.\n\nThe final result of shade steps based on this method are:\n\n- 0\n- 20\n- 40\n- 100\n- 180\n- 280\n- 398\n- 520\n- 590\n- 700\n- 820\n- 930\n- 960\n- 990\n- 1000\n\n  \n\n\nAt first look this might look like an arbitrary selection of shade steps with no real rhythm or rhyme to it. But these steps have been intentionally and precisely chosen.  To propose the specific shade steps in this specification we have done an exercise of figuring out the color needs that we have / will have in our Design system. \n\nWe have defined the following different types of color needs that we have when we design UIs:\n\n- **Background**\n  - Background layer 1\n  - Background layer 2\n  - Background layer 3\n- **Foreground**\n  - Primary foreground\n  - Secondary foreground\n- **Border**\n  - Primary border\n  - Secondary border\n  - Highlight border\n- **Text**\n  - Primary text\n  - Secondary text\n  - Interactive text\n  - Text on primary foreground\n  - Text on secondary foreground\n\n  \n\n\nThese types of color needs all have different contrast requirements to each other (see [law](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vL0W-MA5QOLnQ_yZMt1xJ-tsVmiy5UD8613XCnpdi0w/edit?tab=t.0#heading=h.lw4l5xxm4acn) section). We created a custom tool where we encoded these types of color needs and all contrast requirements between them. This helped us to move around the colors within the bounds of the legal requirements and helped us strike a balance between abiding by the law and meeting brand needs. Using this methodology we arrived at the shade steps defined above which should perfectly match our current UI needs.\n\n#### Shade steps for ephemeral states (i.e. hover, active, disabled etc.)\n\nShade steps for ephemeral states will be created dynamically by manipulating the lightness values of the colors in the palette and they will therefore not be part of the main palette.\n\n## **Target color space**\n\nA color space is a specific organization of colors, which helps in the reproduction of color in both physical and digital realms. It defines a range of colors that can be represented and manipulated.\n\n​\n\nThere are two color spaces that come into question for this color palette:\n\n- [srgb](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/SRGB)\n- [Display P3](https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DCI-P3)\n\n  \n\n\nSrgb is the color space that has been the standard on the web until now and almost every device today can display srgb colors completely. Display P3 is a more modern color space that contains about 25% more colors than srgb. [Display P3 has baseline support since 2023](https://caniuse.com/css-color-function) and it is becoming apparent that [Display P3 will be the next web standard](https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/8659#issuecomment-1489160030). \n\n​\n\n[We have created a metric on Google Analytics](https://analytics.google.com/analytics/web/#/analysis/p253146907/edit/SzNReRP4RpWaFJuTgVDR6A) for ubuntu.com and there we can see that about 88% of our users can display srgb, about 12% can display Display P3 and a negligible proportion cannot even fully display srgb.\n\n​\n\nThis means that although a significant proportion of our users can already display Display P3, it does not make sense to use Display P3 as the target color space for this version of the color palette. \n\n## **Working color gamut**\n\nA color gamut is the complete range of colors that can be represented in a specific color space. It defines the limits of color reproduction for that space.\n\n​\n\nFor creating the color palette we want to work in a perceptually uniform color gamut (such as [OkLch](https://bottosson.github.io/posts/oklab/)). Perceptually uniform in this case essentially means that the color gamut is modelled according to human color perception. This is in contrast to RGB for example which is modelled to make it easy for computers to display colors.\n\nFor example a 10-point lightness increase should feel the same whether applied to yellow or red. In other, RGB based, color gamut such as HSL this is not the case and you might get widely different brightness jumps for different hues, for the same 10-point lightness change.\n\n​\n\nWhat this means is that perceptually uniform color gamut such as OKLch lend themselves a lot better to creating color palettes that appear to be visually consistent from a human perception.\n\n​\n\nFor this color palette we have chosen to work with [Okhsl](https://bottosson.github.io/posts/colorpicker/)\n\n- Okhsl is based on Oklab but tries to fix the out-of-gamut problem of Oklch. By doing that it is not truly perceptually uniform anymore. It tries to strike a tradeoff between ease of use from traditional color gamut like HSL and the perception accuracy from Oklab / Oklch.\n- This tradeoff is favorable as color palette generation is significantly simpler with this tradeoff.\n- Okhsl only supports srgb but it [seems to be relatively simple to expand it to Display P3](https://github.com/w3c/csswg-drafts/issues/8659#issuecomment-1500699972), [Source code for Okhsl-P3](https://gist.github.com/facelessuser/2315c37741ae5253b8b42d2d41bcfa5a)\n\n  \n\n\nTo evaluate the actual difference between colors we can still use OkLch.\n\n## **Unit**\n\nWhile the colors that we create for this first version of the color specification are within the srgb color space we would ideally like to use a color gamut that is able to encode Display P3 colors as well. So that when we want to expand our color space in the future we don’t need to change the color gamut / our unit. Additionally it makes sense to pick a unit which is human readable/understandable, where the components of the unit map to human understandable properties of color (lightness, chroma, hue) and the rate of change for those properties matches human perception too (perceptually uniform color gamut). \n\n​\n\nFor these reasons we have chosen OKLch as the color gamut / unit to represent our colors. It is perceptually uniform, can encode Display P3 colors and is supported by the [W3C Design token specification](https://www.designtokens.org/tr/drafts/color/#oklch) as well as having [native support in CSS](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/CSS/color_value/oklch).\n\n​\n\n## **Interactive states**\n\nInteractive states such as hover, active and disabled are not represented as dedicated shades on the color palette. Adding them as palette shades would have introduced three additional steps per hue, bloating the palette with very subtle lightness differences that serve only a narrow purpose. Instead, interactive states are derived dynamically by manipulating the OKLch lightness and chroma components of the base color. This is possible because we represent our colors in OKLch: its lightness channel can be adjusted directly to produce perceptually consistent state changes across every hue and shade.\n\n​\n\nThese adjusted lightness values are stored as primitive number tokens (e.g. number.lightness.foreground.primary.hover.onLight). Semantic color tokens then reference these number tokens in place of the lightness component, while keeping the chroma and hue of the base color intact. This means a single set of number tokens drives the interactive states for all semantic meanings (destructive, constructive, branded, etc.) that share the same foreground type, because the lightness shift is independent of hue.\n\n​\n\nAdditionally, disabled states use a universal chroma token (number.chroma.disabled, set to 0.1) that replaces the original chroma value. Reducing chroma desaturates the color, reinforcing the visual impression that the element is inactive. This is applied alongside the lightness adjustment so that disabled elements appear both lighter (or darker in dark mode) and less saturated.\n\n#### Why lightness manipulation over opacity\n\nA common shorthand for disabled states is to reduce the opacity of the element. We deliberately chose direct lightness manipulation instead, for the following reasons:\n\n​\n\n- **Layering issues.** Elements that are positioned absolutely, such as floating action buttons or dropdown menus, sit above other content. Applying opacity to these elements makes the content behind them visible through the element, which is visually undesirable and can harm readability.\n\n  \n\n\n- **Precision.** Opacity reduces apparent lightness as a side effect, but the resulting lightness change depends on the background color beneath the element. The same opacity value produces different perceived lightness shifts on different backgrounds. By manipulating the OKLch lightness channel directly, we achieve a deterministic and consistent result regardless of what sits behind the element.\n\n  \n\n\n- **Independent control of foreground and background.** Opacity affects the entire element uniformly. With direct lightness manipulation we can adjust the foreground fill and the text on that foreground independently, ensuring both remain legible in their disabled state.\n\n#### How the lightness values are calculated\n\nThe number tokens encode OKLch lightness values that are derived from the position of the base color on the palette scale (0–1000). Two different calculation methods are used depending on the state:\n\n**Hover and active**\n\nFor hover and active states, the new palette position is calculated by adding a fixed step offset to the base shade step:\n\n​\n\n**new_step = base_step + offset**\n\n​\n\nFor example, a primary foreground at palette step 520 with a hover offset of +40 on light mode yields a new step of 560. On dark mode the offset is negative (e.g. -40 for hover, -70 for active) so that the color shifts in the opposite direction on the lightness scale. The palette step is then converted to an OKLch lightness value using the inverse of the WCAG relative luminance formula:\n\n​\n\n**lightness = (1.05 / (21 ^ (step / 1000)) - 0.05) ^ (1/3)**\n\n​\n\nThis formula derives from the contrast guarantees built into the palette: shade steps map to relative luminance values, and the cube root converts relative luminance to OKLch lightness. The result is rounded to two decimal places and stored as the token value.\n\n​\n\nThe system uses a single, consistent set of offsets across all element types:\n\n​\n\nTable 88\n\n​\n\nOn light mode the offsets are positive, moving the color to a higher (darker) shade step. On dark mode the offsets are negative, moving the color to a lower (lighter) shade step. In both cases the interactive state shifts the element away from the background and toward the viewer, creating a visual impression of the element responding to interaction.\n\n​\n\nThe active offset is larger than the hover offset so that the progression from default → hover → active produces an escalating visual change that communicates increasing engagement. The active offset is also asymmetric between light (+60) and dark (-70) modes. This accounts for the non-linear relationship between palette steps and perceived lightness: the dark end of the scale is perceptually compressed, so a larger step change is needed to produce an equivalent perceptual shift.\n\n​\n\nThe base step that these offsets are applied to varies per element type depending on its default-state shade (e.g. step 520 for a primary foreground, step 100 for a secondary foreground, step 0 for a ghost foreground on the default background). For elements that can appear on different background layers the base step also shifts to match the layer (e.g. step 20 on layer 2 instead of step 0 on layer 1). The offsets themselves remain the same regardless of the element type or layer.\n\n​\n\nThere are two narrow exceptions to this system:\n\n​\n\n- **Input foregrounds use an active offset of 0.** The active (focused) state of an input is communicated through the focus ring rather than a fill color shift.\n\n  \n\n\n- **The root variant of the primary foreground inverts the offset direction.** This variant uses black (step 1000) on light mode and white (step 0) on dark mode rather than the mid-tone shade used by the semantic meaning variants. Because it already sits at the extreme end of the scale, the offset must go in the opposite direction (-40 for hover on light, +40 for hover on dark) to avoid going out of range and to shift the color toward a visible gray.\n\n**Disabled**\n\nDisabled states cannot use a simple fixed offset because the direction and magnitude of the shift must be relative to the background. A dark element on a light background needs to become lighter (move toward the background), while a light element on a dark background needs to become darker (also move toward the background). Elements that are already close to the background in lightness should shift less than those that are far away. This is achieved using linear interpolation between the base step and the background step:\n\n​\n\n**new_step = base_step + (background_step - base_step) × factor**\n\n​\n\nThe factor is set globally to 0.66, meaning the disabled state moves 66% of the distance from the base color toward the background color on the palette scale. For example, a border at step 520 on a light background (step 0) yields a disabled step of 520 + (0 - 520) × 0.66 = 176.8. On a dark background (step 990) the same border yields 520 + (990 - 520) × 0.66 = 830.2. The resulting step is then converted to an OKLch lightness value using the same formula described above.\n\n​\n\nThis approach ensures that disabled states always move proportionally toward the background, producing a natural \"washed out\" appearance regardless of how light or dark the original element was and regardless of whether the theme is light or dark.\n\n​\n\nThe code used for generating the tokens can be found in the [Code - Interactive states](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vL0W-MA5QOLnQ_yZMt1xJ-tsVmiy5UD8613XCnpdi0w/edit?tab=t.ttw1g6ituoxs) tab.\n\n## How we created semantic tokens\n\nThe semantic color tokens were derived methodically from an analysis of our existing component library (Vanilla) and a survey of established design systems. The goal was to create a layered system that covers the vast majority of color needs through generic, reusable tokens while only introducing component-specific tokens where strictly necessary.\n\n## **Step 1: Defining semantic categories**\n\nWe began by identifying the fundamental types of color roles that recur across UI components. These map directly to the top-level categories in the token files:\n\n​\n\nTable 89\n\n​\n\n## **Step 2: Building the base tokens**\n\nFor each category we first created the base (default state) tokens. For example, color.foreground.primary.$root defines the fill color for a primary button, color.text.$root defines the default body text color and color.border.$root defines the default border color. These base tokens directly reference shades from the primitive color palette (e.g. color.palette.gray.520 for a mid-tone foreground).\n\n## **Step 3: Adding semantic meanings**\n\nEach base token was then extended with variants that carry semantic meaning. These communicate the intent or consequence of an action or state to the user:\n\n​\n\n- **branded** - elements associated with the brand identity (orange hue)\n- **destructive** - actions that delete or remove data (red hue)\n- **constructive** - actions that create or add data (green hue)\n- **error** - indicating a problem or validation failure (red hue)\n- **warning** - indicating something that requires attention (yellow hue)\n- **success** - indicating a positive outcome (green hue)\n- **information** - indicating neutral, informative context (blue hue)\n- **muted** - reduced emphasis for secondary content (lighter/darker gray)\n- **highlighted** - increased emphasis for prominent content (inverted contrast)\n\n  \n\n\nThese semantic variants share the same lightness and chroma structure as their base counterpart but substitute the appropriate hue from the primitive palette. This means the interactive state number tokens (hover, active, disabled lightness values) can be reused across all semantic meanings for a given foreground type.\n\n## **Step 4: Adding interactive states and layers**\n\nEach token that represents an interactive element was extended with hover, active and disabled variants. As described in the interactive states section above, these variants reference number tokens for their lightness component rather than pointing directly at a palette shade.\n\n​\n\nTokens that can appear on different background layers were additionally extended with layer2 and layer3 variants. Because the background lightness differs between layers, the interactive state lightness values need to be calculated relative to the correct background. For example, color.foreground.ghost.layer2.hover references number.lightness.foreground.ghost.hover.onLayer2.onLight rather than the default onLight value.\n\n## **Step 5: Handling component-specific exceptions**\n\nThe system described in steps 1- 4 covers the large majority of our component color needs through generic, reusable tokens. However, a small number of components have unique color requirements that do not map cleanly to the generic categories. Specifically, several form components required dedicated tokens:\n\n​\n\n- **Checkbox** - separate tokens for the checkmark fill, the selected state background and the unselected state border (with hover, active and disabled variants, plus layer variants)\n- **Radio** - similar structure to checkbox with selected/unselected states and layer variants\n- **Switch** - tokens for the knob fill, the selected track and the unselected track (with layer variants)\n- **Slider** - tokens for the progress fill, the track and the track endstop\n- **Input** - tokens for the input foreground with hover, active and disabled states across all three layers\n\n  \n\n\nThese component-specific tokens follow the same principles (referencing number tokens for interactive states, using the same disabled factor and chroma reduction) but have their own base shades and state offsets tuned to the visual requirements of each component.\n\n## **Result**\n\nBy structuring the tokens this way: Generic categories first, semantic meanings second, states and layers third, and component-specific exceptions last we arrive at a system where only a small fraction of the 316 tokens per theme are component-specific. The majority are generic and reusable across any component that needs a foreground, border, text or icon color with a particular semantic meaning and interactive state. This significantly reduces the total number of tokens compared to an approach where every component defines its own full set of color tokens.\n\n​\n\n# **Tokens**\n\nThe tokens for this specification can be found in the [Tokens tab](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vL0W-MA5QOLnQ_yZMt1xJ-tsVmiy5UD8613XCnpdi0w/edit?tab=t.mwhtirevuzgi). They have been moved there because there are quite a lot of them.\n\n# **Future work**\n\n- Revisit hues and chroma\n\n# **Spec History and Changelog**\n\nPlease be thorough when recording changes and progress with the spec itself and the work resulting from it. Record every meeting, attendees and conclusions from the meeting.\n\n  \n\n\n\n\n# References\n\n[Canonical Internal]: [DE051 - Color tokens](https://docs.google.com/document/d/1vL0W-MA5QOLnQ_yZMt1xJ-tsVmiy5UD8613XCnpdi0w/edit?usp=sharing)";
    ds:knownEdgeCases "";
    ds:conceptType ds:concepttype.Explanation.
